{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>A setInterval that checks the DOM 10 times a second, forever</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A third-party widget injects a chat bubble into your page after it loads, and you need to react when it appears, maybe to reposition it. You write <code>setInterval(() => { if (document.querySelector('.widget')) ... }, 100)</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      It works, but now your page is running a DOM query ten times a second, forever, even ten minutes after the widget already appeared and nothing is going to change again.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Polling for a DOM change is wasteful in the common case (nothing changed yet, so every check is empty) and imprecise in the case you actually care about (it can take up to your polling interval to notice a real change).
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      <code>MutationObserver</code> is the browser's own answer to this: instead of the page asking "did anything change?" on a timer, the browser tells the page exactly when something changed, with zero polling overhead in between.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>undefined</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      MutationObserver watches a specific part of the page and calls your function the moment something you asked about changes, instead of you having to keep asking.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      You create an observer with a callback, then call <code>observe()</code> on a target node with a config object describing what to watch: child elements being added or removed, attribute changes, or text content changes.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Internally, mutation observers batch their notifications into microtasks, so many rapid DOM changes in the same tick are delivered as one array of mutation records, not one callback invocation per change, which is part of why they're cheap even on busy pages.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>Waiting for a third-party script that loads on its own schedule</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A page embeds a support-chat widget that injects its own DOM after a script loads asynchronously, sometimes after 200ms, sometimes after 3 seconds depending on the user's connection.
    </p>
    <p>
      The original code polled every 100ms to check if the widget's container existed yet, so it could apply custom styling to it. It worked, but left the interval running even after the widget appeared, and even on pages where the widget failed to load at all.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Switching to a MutationObserver on <code>document.body</code> watching for child-list changes meant the styling code ran exactly once, immediately when the widget's node actually appeared, and the observer was disconnected right after, no wasted checks before or after.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The team also added a timeout fallback, disconnecting the observer and logging a warning if the widget never appeared within 10 seconds, since an observer alone doesn't tell you when to give up waiting for something that may never arrive.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

